# Wireworm

## Overview

Wireworm is a fixed-shooter arcade game for the browser, played on a circuit
board. A segmented data-worm winds down the board through a field of capacitor
nodes. The player is a defrag cursor pinned to a shallow band along the floor,
firing upward to cut the worm apart before it reaches the band.

Wireworm's defining idea is the charged field. Every node the worm is turned by
gains charge, so the collision that steers the worm also arms the terrain it
steers on. A fully charged node detonates when it is shot, arcing through the
charged cluster around it, clearing those nodes and frying every worm segment
caught in the arc. Every segment cut by a bolt leaves a fresh node behind, so
the field thickens as the fight goes on, and a critical node the worm reaches
sends it diving straight down.

Wireworm has its own name, look, and rules, and its assets, branding, and design
are original to it.

The specification spans several files under `specs/`. `specs/board.md` gives the
board's geometry and the tile-to-stage map; `specs/nodes.md` the node field and
its charge; `specs/discharge.md` the chain-arc discharge; `specs/worm.md` the
data-worm; `specs/foes.md` the three support foes; `specs/cursor.md` the cursor
and its bolts; `specs/controls.md` the keys; `specs/progression.md` the run of
lives and levels; `specs/scoring.md` every score figure; `specs/ui.md` the
screens, the HUD, and audio; `specs/assets.md` the seeded sprite art;
`specs/state.md` what the game's state carries; `specs/instrumentation.md` the
debug and automation surface; and `specs/showcase.md` the showcase the finished
build ships. Read all of them and build the game they describe as one cohesive
whole.

