## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions, sizes, speeds, and ranges in this specification are given in the
game's own logical units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H`
(`1280 x 720`, 16:9). The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the
right and `y` increases downward.

An entity's position is its center. Wherever the cursor, a foe, or a bolt is
given, taken, or reported as an `(x, y)` pair, that pair is the center of the
entity rather than the top-left corner of its sprite. The board is a grid of
tiles, and an entity sits squarely on a tile exactly when its center is that
tile's center; the tile it occupies is the one containing its center. The
tile-to-stage map and its inverse are in `specs/board.md`, which owns them.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's work: the uniform scale that preserves the
  aspect ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The
  complete stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any
  pixel density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds,
integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update. The one clocked
quantity is the worm's tile step, which `specs/worm.md` states.

## The screens the build has

The build carries six screens, and the game is on exactly one of them at a time:
`title`, `howto`, `playing`, `paused`, `victory`, and `gameover`. The `playing`
screen runs in three sub-phases, `banner`, `active`, and `respawn`.
`specs/ui.md` defines what each screen shows and where each one leads, and
`specs/progression.md` defines the phases.

## What the board shows

While the game is being played, the board carries every node standing on it at
its own charge, every worm and each of its segments, every foe, every bolt in
flight, the arcs of a discharge while they last, and the cursor inside its band.
The HUD bar above the board carries the score, the lives remaining, and the
level. `specs/board.md` gives the extent of each region and `specs/ui.md` the
readouts.

## Visual design

The board is dark. Choose the colors and the typography, decide how a node, a
worm segment, a foe, the cursor, and a bolt are drawn, and decide what the board
and its floor band look like behind them.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Charge, by level | The four charge states of a node are told apart at a glance, and they read as a ramp: each state is drawn visibly brighter than the one below it, with critical the brightest. The palette is yours. |
| The worm | The worm reads apart from the board behind it and from a node of any charge, and its head, its body, and its tail are told apart. |
| The cursor | The cursor reads apart from the player band it sits in. |
| The foes | The glitch, the dropper, and the corruptor read apart from one another and from the board. |
| A bolt in flight | A bolt reads apart from the board along the column it is climbing. |
| The band | The player band reads as a distinct floor across the full width of the board. |
| Text | Every readout and every screen's text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

The palette, the type, the glow, and every other aspect of the look are yours.
